    formula: "FoS = ultimate strength ÷ applied stress",
    formulaExplanation: "The factor of safety is the ratio of a material's failure strength to the stress actually applied in service. A value of one means the part is loaded exactly to its limit with no margin, while a value of two means it could carry twice the load before failing. Dividing strength by stress expresses how many times over the design can bear the working load, giving a direct measure of the safety margin built in.",
    explanation: "The factor of safety measures how much stronger a component is than it strictly needs to be, and this safety factor calculator finds it by dividing the material's ultimate strength by the stress the part actually experiences in use. A result above one means the design has margin to spare, while a value at or below one warns that the part is loaded to or beyond its limit and is likely to fail.\n\nThis matters in every load-bearing design: bridges, cranes, pressure vessels, aircraft, lifting gear, and machine parts all carry a specified factor of safety set by codes, standards, or engineering judgement. The margin covers uncertainties in loads, material variability, manufacturing flaws, corrosion, and wear over time. A safety factor calculator lets designers confirm that a chosen material and cross-section keep the part comfortably away from failure.\n\nThe formula works because it compares the stress a material can withstand before failing with the stress it actually sees, expressing the result as a simple multiple. A factor of four means the part could take four times its working load before breaking. Typical values range from around 1.5 for well-understood static loads up to 10 or more for critical or uncertain applications. Structural, mechanical, and aerospace engineers rely on a safety factor calculator to verify designs.",
    example: { inputs: { ultimateStrength: 400, appliedStress: 100 }, explanation: "FoS = 400 ÷ 100 = 4, so the part could carry four times the applied stress before failing." },
